{"abstract":"This study explores and advances the Fifth Hand principle of project behavior, a recent theoretical approach that accommodates the contradictory explanations of project behavior—Flyvbjerg’s Planning Fallacy and Hirschman’s Hiding Hand (Ika et al., 2022; Love et al., 2022). While the principle has been conceptually developed, its empirical applicability remains unexplored. This study addresses this gap by analyzing a capacity-building project conducted by the Danish Tax Authority. The findings reveal the coexistence of contradictory project behaviors which leads to the existence of both failure and success in the project depending on the macro- and micro-level perspectives. The study contributes to the advancement of the Fifth Hand principle, by providing an empirical exploration and by offering insights into how contradictory behaviors are accommodated in projects. The contributions are conceived by paying close attention to the lived experience of managing projects in practice.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Boll, Karen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-808124"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Boll, Karen"],"keyword":["Capacity building projects","Fifth hand principle","Hiding hand","Planning fallacy","Project behavior"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"exploring and advancing the fifth hand principle of project behavior lessons from denmark s tax governance project","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijproman.2025.102713","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-808124","identifier":[{"id":"105004875848","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["cbs-f8106f79-a0a8-4c9d-8f90-7678803324d9"],"keyword":["Capacity-building projects","Fifth hand principle","Hiding hand","Planning fallacy","Project behavior"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-21D673DCCDAEB67EA59A1B2018B628AC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/115175715/1-s2.0-S0263786325000390-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijproman.2025.102713","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Organization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Karen","key":"bollpau","last":"Boll","locid":"ea9574f1-23ef-4b7a-956c-dafe20986b54","name":"Boll, Karen","orcid":"0000-0001-9497-4372","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.ijproman.2025.102713","issn":["02637863","18734634"],"issn_org":["02637863"],"issue":"3","journal":"International Journal of Project Management","paperid":"102713","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Project Management","volume":"43"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105004875848"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-f8106f79-a0a8-4c9d-8f90-7678803324d9"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Exploring and Advancing the Fifth Hand Principle of Project Behavior : Lessons From Denmark’s Tax Governance Project","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-808124","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"This paper addresses the way that agile methods, which is a widespread set of methods used to develop digital technologies, unwittingly create institutional displacements over and beyond IT development. Organizational studies on digital transformations in public sector organizations focus on the the use and consequences of technologies, e.g. there is a vivid stream of theorization around the relationship between technologies and organizations. We propose to expand this existing focus by looking at how digital technologies are developed and how this development have organizational consequences. Drawing on Scott and Orlikowski, we conceptualise transformations as linked to a digital undertow. Digital undertow refers to a corollary effect of waves of digitalization that profoundly influences how organizations operate, but which manifest themselves elsewhere; away from the actual uses of technologies. Based on a qualitative case study of the use of agile methods within the Danish Tax Authority, we show that in intraorganizational collaboration these methods unwittingly influence practices of other parts of the organization and form values, norms, and rules centeret around the organization as adaptive, responsive, and flexible. The leads us to argue that agile methods form a digital undertow with enormous organizing power. We urge organization scholars to analyze these methods as they create profound changes that we risk missing-out on when looking at the visible waves of digitalization and the constant new launches of technologies.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Boll, Karen","Fugl-Meyer, Ann"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718376"],"event":{"end":"2024-01-25","place":"Frederiksberg, dk","start":"2024-01-24","title":"21st Scandinavian Workshop on E-Government (SWEG)","year":"2024"},"filters":{"added":"2024-03","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Agile methods","Digital transformation","Digital undertow","Public sector","Tax authority","Technology"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the undertow of agile methods in digital transformations","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-718376","ids":["cbs-fd7871d7-1caf-4749-ab1f-25693a7e9c67"],"keyword":["Agile methods","Digital transformation","Digital undertow","Public sector","Tax authority","Technology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-5E2E48226AC70D5E4563B21DCE13C18E"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Organization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Karen","key":"bollpau","last":"Boll","locid":"ea9574f1-23ef-4b7a-956c-dafe20986b54","name":"Boll, Karen","orcid":"0000-0001-9497-4372","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Ann","key":"fuglmeyerpau","last":"Fugl-Meyer","locid":"1e16217b-3d8b-4903-ba74-92d045fcfd3c","name":"Fugl-Meyer, Ann","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-fd7871d7-1caf-4749-ab1f-25693a7e9c67"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"The Undertow of Agile Methods in Digital Transformations","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718376","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"This chapter explores the disconcertedness of tax inspectors when performing their job of inspecting businesses’ tax compliance. It shows how a group of tax inspectors experience their focus in work being distorted due to the implementation of a new strategic direction set by the tax authority. To analyse this situation, I draw parallels with Graeber’s work on bureaucracy and dead zones to argue that tax inspectors are engaged in jobs that they cannot make sense of, which lowers their job satisfaction and creates opaque success criteria. Methodologically, the chapter is based on in-depth, qualitative interviews with tax inspectors from the Danish Tax Authority, who all express concerns about their new work. Building on this analysis, the chapter also includes a reflexive part where I present material that shows my own previous interpretation of this state-of-affairs and demonstrates how I was exposed to some of the same challenges as the tax inspectors. The chapter explores a core area for the anthropology of tax, that is, that of the changing strategies in tax administration and the effects that this has on the tax inspectors’ work.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Boll, Karen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-774354"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Anthropology of tax","Bullshit jobs","Bureacracy","Bureaucracy","Dead zones","Hypocrisy","Interpretive labor","Job satisfaction","Performance indicators","Strategic direction","Structural violence","Tax authority","Tax inspection","Tax inspectors"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dead zones of tax inspection the new strategic direction in the danish tax authority and its consequences for front staff","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009254571.014","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-774354","ids":["cbs-53161eb7-4cff-4883-9286-a70d2b7196d1"],"keyword":["Anthropology of tax","Bullshit jobs","Bureacracy","Bureaucracy","Dead zones","Hypocrisy","Interpretive labor","Job satisfaction","Performance indicators","Strategic direction","Structural violence","Tax authority","Tax inspection","Tax inspectors"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-48C3A15EB2884FF84D835C21D7552E0B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/110942983/dead-zones-of-tax-inspection-the-new-strategic-direction-in-the-danish-tax-authority-and-its-consequences-for-front-staff.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009254571.014","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Organization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Karen","key":"bollpau","last":"Boll","locid":"ea9574f1-23ef-4b7a-956c-dafe20986b54","name":"Boll, Karen","orcid":"0000-0001-9497-4372","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Johanna","key":"muglerpedh","last":"Mugler","name":"Mugler, Johanna","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Miranda Sheild","key":"johanssonpedh","last":"Johansson","name":"Johansson, Miranda Sheild","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Robin","key":"smithpedh","last":"Smith","name":"Smith, Robin","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1017/9781009254571.014","eisbn":["9781009254571"],"isbn":["9781009254588"],"pages":"284-303","place":"Cambridge","publisher":"Cambridge University Press","status":"p","subtitle":"Ethnographies of Fiscal Relations","title":"Anthropology and Tax"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-53161eb7-4cff-4883-9286-a70d2b7196d1"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Dead Zones of Tax Inspection : The New Strategic Direction in the Danish Tax Authority and Its Consequences for Front Staff","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-774354","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
