{"author_count":3,"contributors":["Boll, Karen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-299399"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"securing tax compliance with collaboration the case of cooperative compliance in denmark","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-299399","ids":["cbs-fb5f57bf-b253-43b5-8c21-b71b46c6dd13"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-5F88D88AE313404E17F0F0F859128DBE"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Organization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Karen","key":"bollpau","last":"Boll","locid":"ea9574f1-23ef-4b7a-956c-dafe20986b54","name":"Boll, Karen","orcid":"0000-0001-9497-4372","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Nigar","key":"hashimzadepedh","last":"Hashimzade","name":"Hashimzade, Nigar","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Yuliya","key":"epifantsevapedh","last":"Epifantseva","name":"Epifantseva, Yuliya","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9781315673745"],"isbn":["9781138941342"],"pages":"212-224","place":"Abingdon","publisher":"Routledge","series":"Routledge Companions in Business, Management and Accounting","status":"p","title":"Routledge Companion to Tax Avoidance Research"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85135646863"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-fb5f57bf-b253-43b5-8c21-b71b46c6dd13"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Securing Tax Compliance with Collaboration : The Case of Cooperative Compliance in Denmark","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-299399","year":"2018","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"For the last decade a major trend within tax administrations has been to shift from a roughly one size fits all approach—where close to all taxpayers experience a deterrence approach—to a more responsive and collaborative approach as in co-operative compliance programmes. Such programmes build on the idea that the participating corporations disclose relevant information including their tax risks and are transparent to the tax administrations and in return will tax administrations provide real-time predictability and clarity concerning taxation issues of relevance for the corporation. In brief, co-operative compliance builds on the slogan: “…certainty in exchange for transparency” (OECD 2016, 7). Co-operative compliance has increasingly become a core concern and way of organizing the relation between tax authorities and large corporate tax payers when it comes to securing tax compliance. This working paper is the result of research by Work Package 6 in EU’s Horizon 2020 funded programme FairTax that has been running for the four-year period 2015-2019. Our research in Work Package 6 addresses how proactive engagements with large corporate taxpayers have affected regulation of tax collection and administrative processes, changed relationships between stakeholders and tax administrations, and influenced tax compliance in the Nordic countries. The aim of this working paper is to provide a comparison of the experiences in four of the Nordic countries: Denmark, Finland, Norway and Sweden and to propose recommendations. The Nordic countries are considered similar and so were the co-operative compliance programmes that were implemented in each country, yet the outcomes were very different. We thus dealt with various case characteristics (Flyvbjerg 2006) where the outcomes hinged on a complexity of elements. We argue that the Swedish case is an extreme case due to its turbulent life and concomitantly with only a handful of participants that have very little activity. The Norwegian case, in contrast, is an example of a maximum variation case because of the much longer history of collaborative relationships and the outcome of the work with tax risk. The combination of a collaborative way of working and systematic risk management and monitoring may either reflect a most likely scenario of future tax administration—or perhaps the least likely. Lastly, we argue that the Danish and Finnish cases represent paradigmatic cases because both of these align largely with the standards set by the OECD and because they therefore present more ordinary or regular ways of working with co-operative compliance. Analyzing a wide variety of case characteristics means that our findings can be of general interest, beyond the Nordic countries.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Björklund Larsen, Lotta","Boll, Karen","Brögger, Benedicte","Kettunen, Jaana","Potka-Soininen, Tuulia","Pellinen, Jukka","Johansen, Mette Brehm","Aziz, Kiran"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-298502"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Finland","Norway","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","BI Norwegian Business School","Linköping University","University of Jyväskylä"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"nordic experiences of co operative compliance programmes comparisons and recommendations","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dw","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/58519075/FairTax_WP20.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-298502","ids":["cbs-f2285ef9-31d1-4854-91f1-185cbb6cb46a"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-D2CC98DE15460689B23A93B014E4A0B5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/58519075/FairTax_WP20.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"linköping university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Linköping University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Organization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"no","key":"bi norwegian business school","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"BI Norwegian Business School","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"fi","key":"university of jyväskylä","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Jyväskylä","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lotta","key":"björklundlarsenpau","last":"Björklund Larsen","name":"Björklund Larsen, Lotta","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Karen","key":"bollpau","last":"Boll","locid":"ea9574f1-23ef-4b7a-956c-dafe20986b54","name":"Boll, Karen","orcid":"0000-0001-9497-4372","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Benedicte","key":"bröggerpau","last":"Brögger","name":"Brögger, Benedicte","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Jaana","key":"kettunenpau","last":"Kettunen","name":"Kettunen, Jaana","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Tuulia","key":"potkasoininenpau","last":"Potka-Soininen","name":"Potka-Soininen, Tuulia","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Jukka","key":"pellinenpau","last":"Pellinen","name":"Pellinen, Jukka","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mette","key":"brehmjohansenpau","last":"Brehm Johansen","locid":"e6c36e8a-6bcd-4444-93ed-ff898aef9379","name":"Johansen, Mette Brehm","name_org":"Brehm Johansen, Mette","orcid":"0000-0003-0335-2653","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Kiran","key":"azizpau","last":"Aziz","name":"Aziz, Kiran","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issue":"20","pages":"135","place":"Umeå","publisher":"Umeå University","series":"FairTax - Working Paper Series","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-f2285ef9-31d1-4854-91f1-185cbb6cb46a"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Nordic Experiences of Co-Operative Compliance Programmes : Comparisons and Recommendations","type":"Working Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-298502","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"This chapter focuses on the use of outcome-based performance management systems within public administration. It reports two qualitative case studies from respectively the Danish Tax and Customs Administration and the Swedish Tax Agency. Both of these administrations use outcome-based performance management systems to steer subsets of their administrative work. The chapter shows that the systems respond to broader demands for accounting for outcomes, yet, the systems also operate in very different ways. The Danish case shows a quantitative system which measures on a daily basis, the Swedish case shows a qualitative system which measures on a four to five-year basis. What is striking about both cases is that they balance meeting the demands for accounting for diffuse outcomes, with developing measurements that ‘fit’ local contingent concerns. While much of the current research on performance management systems in public administration is critical and stresses the downsides of such systems, this chapter shows that these systems should not always be assumed to be connected to gaming, strategic behaviour and/or reductionism. Instead, the performance management systems can be seen as attempts to reconcile and make ends meet in ‘post-bureaucratic’ organisations that are increasingly expected to account for rather diffuse and abstract outcomes and expected at the same time to steer and prioritise daily administrative work.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Boll, Karen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-283129"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Denmark","Outcomes","Public administration","Sweden","Tax administration"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"outcome based performance management systems experiences from the danish and swedish tax agencies","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-283129","identifier":[{"id":"85065470581","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["cbs-5167c9d6-130c-43c5-8711-d34097d74dee"],"keyword":["Denmark","Outcomes","Performance management system","Performance management systems","Public administration","Sweden","Tax administration"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-88F297173342B0A6C248F643DEA1769F"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Organization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Karen","key":"bollpau","last":"Boll","locid":"ea9574f1-23ef-4b7a-956c-dafe20986b54","name":"Boll, Karen","orcid":"0000-0001-9497-4372","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Haldor","key":"byrkjeflotpedh","last":"Byrkjeflot","name":"Byrkjeflot, Haldor","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Fredrik","key":"engelstadpedh","last":"Engelstad","name":"Engelstad, Fredrik","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1108/s0195-631020180000033009","eisbn":["9781787432833"],"isbn":["9781787432840"],"pages":"89-107","place":"Bingley","publisher":"Emerald Group Publishing","status":"p","title":"Bureaucracy and Society in Transition"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85065470581"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-5167c9d6-130c-43c5-8711-d34097d74dee"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Outcome-based Performance Management Systems : Experiences from the Danish and Swedish Tax Agencies","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-283129","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"This working paper presents an analysis of the experiences of Cooperative Compliance in Denmark. Cooperative Compliance denotes a specific kind of collaborative program for the regulation of large corporate taxpayers by the tax authorities. Cooperative Compliance programs have been implemented in several countries worldwide. In Denmark the program is called Tax Governance. Tax Governance has been studied using qualitative method and the analyses of the working paper build on an extensive base of in-depth interviews – primarily with tax directors from corporations participating in the program. The working paper shows as a general stance that the corporations are supporting the ideas behind Tax Governance and are generally satisfied with their participation. However, the working paper also shows that most of them explain to be stretched between this willingness to participate and the different challenges and contradictions they told to experience in the everyday work practices related to the Tax Governance program. The working paper zooms in at these various everyday experiences from the corporations. Yet, it also zooms out and shows that the Tax Governance program in different ways relate to wider international trends within tax administration, especially concerning the development of risk assessments and internal control in the corporations and a greater focus on monitoring of these elements by the tax authorities. Overall, the working paper concludes that Tax Governance as a model for a collaborative regulatory relationship between Skat and large corporations comes with both possibilities and challenges.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Boll, Karen","Johansen, Mette Brehm"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-286970"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cooperative compliance","Large corporations","Qualitative interview","Taxation"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"tax governance corporate experiences with cooperative compliance in denmark","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dw","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/58539325/Boll_Brehm_Johansen_Tax_Governance.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-286970","ids":["cbs-795d622d-be44-4088-97de-56358d50790a"],"keyword":["Cooperative compliance","Large corporations","Qualitative interview","Taxation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-4255B3AEB72B1D4E61B4AF3EB59B79E1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://research-api.cbs.dk/ws/files/58539325/Boll_Brehm_Johansen_Tax_Governance.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Organization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Karen","key":"bollpau","last":"Boll","locid":"ea9574f1-23ef-4b7a-956c-dafe20986b54","name":"Boll, Karen","orcid":"0000-0001-9497-4372","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mette","key":"brehmjohansenpau","last":"Brehm Johansen","locid":"e6c36e8a-6bcd-4444-93ed-ff898aef9379","name":"Johansen, Mette Brehm","name_org":"Brehm Johansen, Mette","orcid":"0000-0003-0335-2653","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issue":"17","pages":"112","place":"Umeå","publisher":"Umeå University","series":"FairTax - Working Paper Series","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","Copenhagen Business School","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["cbs-795d622d-be44-4088-97de-56358d50790a"],"src":["CBS Copenhagen Business School"],"title":"Tax Governance : Corporate Experiences with Cooperative Compliance in Denmark","type":"Working Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-286970","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"author_count":10,"contributors":["Boll, Erik J.","Marti, Roger","Hasman, Henrik","Overballe-Petersen, Søren","Stegger, Marc","Ng, Kim","Knøchel, Susanne","Krogfelt, Karen Angeliki","Hummerjohann, Joerg","Struve, Carsten"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-492886"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Biofilms","E coli","Food production","Heat resistance","clpK"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"turn up the heat food and clinical em escherichia coli em isolates feature two transferrable loci of heat resistance","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2017.00579","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-492886","identifier":[{"id":"PMC5383660","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85018283492","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000398617200001","type":"wos"},{"id":"28439262","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-a6821fb6-5aaa-4bbc-8025-27e3c1788dc9"],"keyword":["E. coli","biofilms","clpK","food production","heat resistance","transfer of heat resistance"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-36B72C918028D6C066768DD155B06BFC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2017.00579","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/203412887/fmicb_08_00579.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"ssi statens serum institut","l2":"DI-GV","l2n":"Governmental Institutions","l3":"DI-GV-SSI","l3nn":"SSI Statens Serum Institut","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SSI Statens Serum Institut","namevar":["Statens Serum Institut"],"nora_code":"DI-GV-SSI","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Food Science, Food Microbiology, Gut Health, and Fermentation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Erik J.","key":"bollpau","last":"Boll","name":"Boll, Erik J.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Roger","key":"martipau","last":"Marti","name":"Marti, Roger","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Henrik","key":"hasmanpau","last":"Hasman","name":"Hasman, Henrik","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Søren","key":"overballepetersenpau","last":"Overballe-Petersen","name":"Overballe-Petersen, Søren","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Marc","key":"steggerpau","last":"Stegger","name":"Stegger, Marc","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kim","key":"ngpau","last":"Ng","name":"Ng, Kim","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Susanne","key":"knøchelpau","last":"Knøchel","locid":"41a18653-7b8f-4399-b814-e7782826d4a6","name":"Knøchel, Susanne","orcid":"0000-0003-3777-7123","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Karen Angeliki","key":"krogfeltpau","last":"Krogfelt","name":"Krogfelt, Karen Angeliki","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Joerg","key":"hummerjohannpau","last":"Hummerjohann","name":"Hummerjohann, Joerg","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Carsten","key":"struvepau","last":"Struve","name":"Struve, Carsten","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["28439262"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/fmicb.2017.00579","issn":["1664302X"],"journal":"Frontiers in Microbiology","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Microbiology","volume":"8"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85018283492"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-a6821fb6-5aaa-4bbc-8025-27e3c1788dc9"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Turn Up the Heat-Food and Clinical <em>Escherichia coli</em> Isolates Feature Two Transferrable Loci of Heat Resistance","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-492886","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"X-ray free-electron lasers enable the investigation of the structure and dynamics of diverse systems, including atoms, molecules, nanocrystals and single bioparticles, under extreme conditions. Many imaging applications that target biological systems and complex materials use hard X-ray pulses with extremely high peak intensities (exceeding 10 20 watts per square centimetre). However, fundamental investigations have focused mainly on the individual response of atoms and small molecules using soft X-rays with much lower intensities. Studies with intense X-ray pulses have shown that irradiated atoms reach a very high degree of ionization, owing to multiphoton absorption, which in a heteronuclear molecular system occurs predominantly locally on a heavy atom (provided that the absorption cross-section of the heavy atom is considerably larger than those of its neighbours) and is followed by efficient redistribution of the induced charge. In serial femtosecond crystallography of biological objects - an application of X-ray free-electron lasers that greatly enhances our ability to determine protein structure - the ionization of heavy atoms increases the local radiation damage that is seen in the diffraction patterns of these objects and has been suggested as a way of phasing the diffraction data. On the basis of experiments using either soft or less-intense hard X-rays, it is thought that the induced charge and associated radiation damage of atoms in polyatomic molecules can be inferred from the charge that is induced in an isolated atom under otherwise comparable irradiation conditions. Here we show that the femtosecond response of small polyatomic molecules that contain one heavy atom to ultra-intense (with intensities approaching 10 20 watts per square centimetre), hard (with photon energies of 8.3 kiloelectronvolts) X-ray pulses is qualitatively different: our experimental and modelling results establish that, under these conditions, the ionization of a molecule is considerably enhanced compared to that of an individual heavy atom with the same absorption cross-section. This enhancement is driven by ultrafast charge transfer within the molecule, which refills the core holes that are created in the heavy atom, providing further targets for inner-shell ionization and resulting in the emission of more than 50 electrons during the X-ray pulse. Our results demonstrate that efficient modelling of X-ray-driven processes in complex systems at ultrahigh intensities is feasible.","author_count":34,"contributors":["Rudenko, A.","Inhester, L.","Hanasaki, K.","Li, X.","Robatjazi, S. J.","Erk, B.","Boll, R.","Toyota, Kenjiro","Hao, Y.","Vendrell, Oriol","Bomme, Cédric","Savelyev, Evgeny","Rudek, B.","Foucar, L.","Southworth, S. H.","Lehmann, C. S.","Kraessig, B.","Marchenko, T.","Simon, M.","Ueda, K.","Ferguson, K. R.","Bucher, Marcel","Gorkhover, Tais","Carron, S.","Alonso-Mori, Roberto","Koglin, Jason E","Correa, Karen J.","Williams, Garth J","Boutet, Sébastien","Young, L T","Bostedt, C.","Son, S.-K.","Santra, R.","Rolles, D."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-135655"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Rudenko, A."],"country":["China","France","Germany","Japan","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Argonne National Laboratory","Beijing University of Technology","Fisk University","French National Centre for Scientific Research (CNRS)","Helmholtz Association of German Research Centres","Kansas State University","Northwestern University","Pacific Northwest National Laboratory","SLAC National Accelerator Laboratory","Sorbonne Nouvelle University","Sorbonne Université","Technical University of Berlin","Tohoku University","Universite Paris 5","Universite Paris-Saclay","University of Chicago","University of Hamburg","University of Marburg"],"keyword":["ATOMS","CHARGE","Free 